# Quasar single-host deployment — environment variables.
# Copy to deploy/.env and fill in the required values.
#   cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env

# ── Required ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These three are `:?`-required by docker-compose.yml interpolation — compose
# refuses to start without them. If you run `deploy/redeploy.sh` against a
# fresh deploy/.env (no file, or missing one of these), it generates
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD and ENROLLMENT_TOKEN for you (openssl rand, appended with a
# comment block same as below) and fails BEFORE the image build if anything
# is still missing afterward — safe to leave unset here if redeploy.sh is
# doing the deploy. Set them yourself only if you deploy some other way, or
# want a specific value.

# Both ship COMMENTED OUT deliberately: an active placeholder here would pass
# redeploy.sh's presence checks and deploy with a publicly-known credential
# (the enrollment token authenticates node registration). Leave them commented
# to get generated values, or uncomment with real ones. redeploy.sh refuses
# the literal placeholder values as a backstop.

# Postgres password.  At least 20 random characters recommended.
# Auto-generated by deploy/redeploy.sh (openssl rand -hex 24) when absent.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-strong-password

# The fleet-wide static enrollment token.  Optional on the control plane since
# #12 (unset = only minted per-host tokens can enroll), but the single-host
# agent (dialling ws://localhost) enrolls with it on first boot, so keep it here.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Auto-generated by deploy/redeploy.sh (openssl rand -hex 32) when absent.
#
# Treat it as break-glass: it can enroll ANY node name.  For a SECOND host, do
# not copy this value across — mint a per-host token in Admin -> Fleet ->
# Enroll host and run the one-line installer it prints on that host (or set
# the enrollment string as its QUASAR_ENROLLMENT; agent-link block further down).
# ENROLLMENT_TOKEN=change-me-enrollment-token

# ── Strongly recommended ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not compose-`:?`-required — the control plane boots fine without it — but
# leaving it unset silently disables a whole feature class (admin-UI-stored
# credentials, e.g. the cover-artwork provider key) with nothing louder than
# one INFO line at boot. Set it before you go to production.
#
# Master key for credentials set from the ADMIN UI. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# BACK THIS UP WITH YOUR DEPLOYMENT SECRETS, separately from this file. If you
# lose it, every value stored through the admin UI becomes UNRECOVERABLE and
# must be re-entered — there is no way to decrypt them without the exact key
# that encrypted them. Rotating to a new key does not throw the old values
# away by itself: keep the old key in QUASAR_SECRET_KEY_PREVIOUS (see below)
# so existing rows keep decrypting while new writes use the new key, then
# re-save each secret through the admin UI to re-encrypt it under the new key.
# Full detail: docs/configuration.md §"Encrypted secrets".
# QUASAR_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Optional — override defaults ──────────────────────────────────────────────

# Optional — pre-provision the first admin account at startup on a fresh DB
# (all three required together if you set any). Not compose-required: the
# first-run setup wizard can claim the founding admin instead, so leave these
# unset to do that interactively. After an admin exists (either way), new
# admins must be promoted via the admin API. NOT auto-generated by
# redeploy.sh — the wizard is the intended path for these.
# BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
# BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
# BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-admin-password

# HTTP port the control-plane listens on (and maps to the host).
# CONTROL_PORT=8080

# ── HTTPS / TLS (issue #376) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The control-plane serves the SAME app over HTTPS on a second listener so the
# browser gets a secure context — required for in-game Esc capture (Keyboard
# Lock API) and Gamepad controller support. The plaintext HTTP listener is
# unchanged (the node-agent and healthchecks still use it).
#
# Default (QUASAR_TLS=auto): a self-signed cert is generated on first boot and
# persisted in the quasar-control-tls volume, so it survives restarts. Your
# browser shows a ONE-TIME warning on first visit — accept the exception and the
# page becomes a secure context. Regenerating every boot would re-prompt, hence
# the volume.
#
# !! SET QUASAR_TLS_HOSTS IF YOU WILL REACH THIS BOX OVER THE LAN. The cert's
# SANs are localhost, the loopback IPs, QUASAR_PUBLIC_HOST, whatever is in
# QUASAR_TLS_HOSTS, and the control-plane process's own interface addresses. That
# last set is NOT your LAN IP: the control-plane runs in a container, so all it
# can enumerate is its docker bridge address (172.x.y.z). With no
# QUASAR_TLS_HOSTS, https://<this-host's-lan-ip>:8443 fails hostname validation
# (ERR_CERT_COMMON_NAME_INVALID). That is a NAME mismatch rather than a trust
# failure, so trusting the cert does not make the interstitial go away. Set it to
# the LAN IP/hostname you type in the browser. deploy/seed-tls-hosts.sh fills it
# in from this host's primary LAN address (deploy/redeploy.sh runs it for you);
# run it before your first `docker compose up -d` if you bring the stack up by
# hand.
#
# The pair is generated ONCE and reused for ~10 years, so adding a name here
# AFTER first boot changes nothing until you re-issue: delete cert.pem + key.pem
# from the TLS volume and recreate the control-plane container. That mints a new
# leaf with a NEW fingerprint, so every client that trusted the old cert (browser
# exception or OS/browser trust store) has to trust the new one again.
#
# Set QUASAR_TLS=off for HTTP only (today's behaviour). For a REAL, browser-
# trusted cert, either point QUASAR_TLS_CERT/QUASAR_TLS_KEY at PEM files
# (bind-mount them in), or use the hardened Caddy overlay
# (docker-compose.hardened.yml) which does ACME/real certs on :443.
#
# QUASAR_TLS=auto
# QUASAR_TLS_PORT=8443            # host port for HTTPS. Set BOTH this and
                                  # CONTROL_PORT when the box's low ports are
                                  # taken (e.g. 18080 / 18443) — there is no
                                  # per-host compose file to carry them.
# QUASAR_TLS_HOSTS=play.lan,192.0.2.10   # SAN hostnames/IPs (comma list) — see above
# QUASAR_PUBLIC_HOST=play.lan    # also added as a cert SAN when set
# QUASAR_TLS_CERT=/certs/fullchain.pem  # operator cert (set WITH _KEY)
# QUASAR_TLS_KEY=/certs/privkey.pem     # operator key  (set WITH _CERT)
#
# Browser origins allowed to open the signaling WebSocket (/v1/signal). Leave it
# unset and only same-origin requests are allowed — correct when browsers hit the
# control-plane directly. A reverse proxy in front (your own Caddy/nginx, not the
# hardened overlay, which sets this itself) changes the browser's Origin to the
# proxy's name, so the proxied origin has to be listed here or every session
# launch fails at signaling. List EVERY origin that must work — adding the public
# name without the LAN ones turns off direct https://<lan-ip>:<tls-port> access.
# Exact scheme://host[:port] entries; a malformed one fails startup.
# QUASAR_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://play.example.com,https://192.0.2.10:18443
#
# Networks whose X-Forwarded-For the control plane trusts. Set this to your
# reverse proxy's network whenever one fronts the stack: without it the login,
# signaling, agent-enrollment and setup-claim rate limiters all key on the
# proxy's address, so every client shares one failure budget and an attacker can
# burn the first-admin claim budget with bad tokens and lock you out. Unset is
# safe (the header is never consulted, so nobody can spoof a limiter key) but
# coarse. Name only networks you operate — anyone inside one gets unlimited
# budget. Comma-separated CIDRs; /0 is refused at startup, wider than /8 warns.
# QUASAR_TRUSTED_PROXIES=192.0.2.10/32
#
# EXPERIMENTAL, UNSUPPORTED, OFF BY DEFAULT. ICE servers handed to the browser
# (#509). Leave it unset. Unset is the default and the only supported shape: the
# browser offers host candidates only, which is all a LAN or a VPN that joins
# both ends needs. It is listed here because the variable exists and is read at
# startup, not because it is a capability Quasar offers. Reference:
# docs/configuration.md.
# QUASAR_ICE_SERVERS=
#
# Set this to the URL your users actually type when a reverse proxy fronts the
# stack. Besides the invite magic link it fixes the signaling address handed to
# proxied clients: that is normally derived from the request's Host header, so a
# proxy that rewrites Host to its upstream makes the control plane advertise its
# own private listener, and the browser then dials that instead of the proxy and
# never connects. Only the scheme + host are used, and a request with no
# X-Forwarded-* header keeps its own Host, so direct LAN access is unaffected.
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://play.example.com

# Stable name for this host in the Quasar DB.
# NODE_NAME=quasar-node-1

# ── Agent link (only for a host that is NOT the control plane's machine) ─────
# The single-host default dials ws://localhost and needs none of this.  On a
# second GPU host, paste the enrollment string from Admin -> Fleet -> Enroll
# host.  It carries the control plane's wss:// URL, the fingerprint of its
# certificate (pinned — compare it against the control plane's startup log
# before you paste), and a single-use token that expires in an hour.  The pin
# is saved beside the node secret on first connect, so the string can be
# removed afterwards.
# QUASAR_ENROLLMENT=qenr1.AA:BB:...:FF.d3NzOi8v....<token>
#
# Manual alternative / rotation path: URL + fingerprint + token separately.
# CONTROL_PLANE_URL=wss://cp.example.com:8443
# CONTROL_PLANE_FINGERPRINT=AA:BB:...:FF
#
# A ws:// URL to another machine is refused (credentials would cross the wire
# in cleartext).  Only on a network you own end to end, and only as a
# transition:
# QUASAR_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_AGENT=1

# Postgres user (default: quasar).
# POSTGRES_USER=quasar

# Bearer token lifetime (Go duration string). Default: 24h.
# AUTH_TOKEN_TTL=24h

# Log level: debug | info | warn | error.  Default: info.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# ── GPU / encoder ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Encoder. Unset, the agent auto-detects the GPU vendor and picks the default
# itself: NVIDIA/AMD -> "vulkan" (the Vulkan path is immune to the #489 NVENC
# driver crash), Intel -> "va", no GPU -> "openh264". Set a value to override:
# "va" (AMD/Intel VA-API), "vulkan", "nvenc" (explicit opt-out back to the
# NVENC path on an NVIDIA host), "openh264" (software, always works, no GPU).
# QUASAR_ENCODER=va

# Render node for the Wayland compositor. Only needed on a box with more than
# one GPU: unset means any GPU on the host is eligible and a session binds to
# whichever the scheduler picks (single-GPU hosts need nothing here). A
# /dev/dri/by-path/... selector survives reboots and PCI reordering, a bare
# renderDNNN does not.
# QUASAR_RENDER_NODE=/dev/dri/by-path/pci-0000:01:00.0-render

# !! Hardware encode needs BOTH of these, or the encoder is silently absent:
#   1. /dev/dri is uncommented in docker-compose.yml devices:.
#   2. LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE and MESA_LOADER_DRIVER_OVERRIDE stay UNSET.
#      They are unset by default. Setting MESA_LOADER_DRIVER_OVERRIDE=softpipe
#      makes Mesa load the software gallium driver even for a real DRM node, so
#      the va plugin probes a software device and registers no vah264enc. The
#      node-agent exits at startup with a clear error if it detects the conflict.
#
# For a SOFTWARE-ONLY host (no GPU), set all three:
# QUASAR_ENCODER=openh264
# LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1
# MESA_LOADER_DRIVER_OVERRIDE=softpipe

# Software Mesa overrides — only needed to DISABLE the softpipe defaults above.
# Leave commented out unless you are using QUASAR_ENCODER=openh264.
# LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1
# MESA_LOADER_DRIVER_OVERRIDE=softpipe

# ── Managed-home storage (P5) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where per-(user, app) persistent homes live: host directories under
# QUASAR_HOME_ROOT ({root}/{user}/{app}). The path must exist at the same
# location on every node-agent host. Any PERSISTENT path works — on
# unraid-style appliance OSes /var/lib is RAM-backed and lost on reboot, so
# use the persistent data share there.
#   unraid:        QUASAR_HOME_ROOT=/mnt/cache/appdata/quasar/homes
#   generic linux: QUASAR_HOME_ROOT=/var/lib/quasar/homes
#
# deploy/redeploy.sh SEEDS QUASAR_HOME_ROOT=/var/lib/quasar/homes on a fresh
# install, so a new deployment gets host-path homes without being asked.
#
# The docker-volume driver was HARD-REMOVED (#473, operator direction
# 2026-08-25): it never had a host path for library discovery to walk, so a
# volume-backed instance never produced quick-launch tiles for installed games
# (#472) — Quasar was unreleased, so removing it outright cost nothing.
# QUASAR_STORAGE_PROVIDER=volume is now rejected at startup / by the admin API.
# QUASAR_HOME_ROOT=
# QUASAR_STORAGE_PROVIDER=   # local; unset = auto (see above) — same behaviour either way

# ── Golden-home templates (#488) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both gates ship OFF. QUASAR_HOME_TEMPLATES lets a host CONSUME a template
# (seed a cold home from one, ~35 s -> ~7 s first Steam boot);
# QUASAR_TEMPLATE_WARMUP lets it BUILD one, which costs a full session on the
# host's encode slot, so it is opt-in and best set on a subset of hosts.
# QUASAR_TEMPLATE_ROOT must be a SIBLING of QUASAR_HOME_ROOT, on the same
# filesystem, and it is bind-mounted into the agent at the same path by
# docker-compose.yml — if you move QUASAR_HOME_ROOT, set this to its sibling too.
# Full knob list (settle, timeouts, min-free, clone mode): docs/configuration.md.
# QUASAR_HOME_TEMPLATES=1
# QUASAR_TEMPLATE_WARMUP=1
# QUASAR_TEMPLATE_ROOT=/var/lib/quasar/templates

# ── Images: pinning a release ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THESE TWO VARS ARE THE WHOLE RELEASE INSTALL. Set them to the digests from the
# release body and deploy/docker-compose.yml is digest-pinned — there is no
# release overlay to add (docker-compose.release.yml was retired; it existed
# only to substitute these two refs).
#
# Prefer a digest over a :X.Y.Z tag. A tag is a pointer; a pin should not move.
# QUASAR_CONTROL_IMAGE=ghcr.io/accreleus/quasar/quasar-control-plane@sha256:...
# QUASAR_AGENT_IMAGE=ghcr.io/accreleus/quasar/quasar-node-agent@sha256:...
#
# Unset, both default to the :latest local tags, which is what a build-from-source
# host serves.
#
# QUASAR_NODE_IMAGE is the older name for QUASAR_AGENT_IMAGE and is still
# honoured, so an existing host keeps working; QUASAR_AGENT_IMAGE wins when both
# are set. Use QUASAR_AGENT_IMAGE in new configuration.
# QUASAR_NODE_IMAGE=quasar-node-agent:latest
#
# ── The updater ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The per-host actor that applies a release. It is NOT part of a release and is
# therefore named by TAG, not by digest: it is what moves the two vars above, so
# it cannot be one of the images they name. Updating it is a separate manual
# step (docs/upgrading.md).
# QUASAR_UPDATER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/accreleus/quasar/quasar-updater:latest
#
# QUASAR_STACK_DIR is this directory's absolute HOST path, mounted into the
# updater at that same path. REQUIRED for the updater to work at all: it
# rebuilds its compose invocation from its own container labels, which record
# host paths. deploy/redeploy.sh seeds it; set it by hand otherwise.
# QUASAR_STACK_DIR=/opt/quasar/deploy
#
# Registry namespaces this host will pull platform images from, comma-separated.
# Unset = ghcr.io/accreleus/quasar. Anything outside them is refused
# `namespace_rejected` before a single byte is pulled.
# QUASAR_UPDATER_ALLOWED_NAMESPACES=ghcr.io/accreleus/quasar
#
# Podman: point this at /run/user/<uid>/podman/podman.sock.
# QUASAR_DOCKER_SOCKET=/var/run/docker.sock
#
# Seconds the updater waits for a recreated service to become healthy.
# QUASAR_UPDATER_WAIT_TIMEOUT_S=300
#
# Release detection (control plane). The stable channel reads GitHub Releases of this
# repo; a fork points QUASAR_PLATFORM_RELEASE_REPO at its own. A token is only needed
# if the unauthenticated GitHub API rate limit ever bites (weekly polling never does).
# The edge channel resolves branch tags on this registry. Every knob, incl. the API
# base and asset-host allowlists, is in docs/configuration.md.
# QUASAR_PLATFORM_RELEASE_REPO=accreleus/quasar
# QUASAR_PLATFORM_RELEASE_TOKEN=
# QUASAR_PLATFORM_REGISTRY=ghcr.io
#
# Hosts a release asset may be downloaded from, and the only hosts its one
# redirect may point at. GitHub moves the CDN it serves assets from, so if a
# release ever fails to record with "redirect host is not allowed", add the host
# the job summary names here.
# QUASAR_PLATFORM_RELEASE_ASSET_HOSTS=github.com,objects.githubusercontent.com,release-assets.githubusercontent.com

# Postgres is pinnable the same way, for an install that wants the exact digest
# the release was validated against.
# QUASAR_POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres:16-alpine
#
# Recommended alongside a pinned install: fail a session outright when the audio
# sidecar is unavailable, instead of silently streaming it silent. Off by default
# so a host with no audio stack still runs. The retired release overlay defaulted
# this to 1; on this path you set it yourself.
# QUASAR_AUDIO_REQUIRED=1

# PulseAudio sidecar image. Defaults to whatever QUASAR_AGENT_IMAGE resolves to —
# the sidecar MUST come from the same runtime lineage or it may have no
# `pulseaudio` binary, which mutes every session (streamed and console-local)
# with only a WARN in the log. Override only if you know why.
# QUASAR_PULSE_IMAGE=

# ── Overlays ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Local display (DRM/KMS console + host audio). Set to 1 AND add
# deploy/overlays/docker-compose.console.yml to the compose chain; deploy/redeploy.sh reads this
# to decide whether to include that overlay.
# QUASAR_CONSOLE=1

# Core-dump capture (deploy/overlays/docker-compose.cores.yml, issue #429). Must be a directory
# on a REAL filesystem — the kernel cannot write a core dump to FUSE, so a share
# path silently produces zero cores. Check with: df -T "$QUASAR_CORE_DIR"
# QUASAR_CORE_DIR=/var/lib/quasar-cores

# Digest-pinned release artifacts (docker-compose.release.yml).
# QUASAR_CONTROL_IMAGE=ghcr.io/example/quasar-control-plane@sha256:...
# QUASAR_AGENT_IMAGE=ghcr.io/example/quasar-agent@sha256:...

# ── Volume adoption (upgrades only) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave these unset on a new deployment: Compose then names volumes
# <project>_<key> as usual. Set them ONLY to adopt volumes that already exist
# under different names — a stack that used to run a forked compose file keeps
# its data instead of starting against an empty database. Read the current names
# with: docker volume ls | grep quasar (or run scripts/dev/migrate-compose-volumes.sh,
# which prints these three lines ready to paste).
#
# !! These only act through the opt-in overlay (#448): Compose v5 rejects an
# empty `name:` default at `up`, so the override lives in
# deploy/overlays/docker-compose.adopt-volumes.yml, not the base file.
# deploy/redeploy.sh adds the overlay AUTOMATICALLY when all three are set
# (and refuses a partial set). MANUAL compose invocations must add it
# themselves:
#   docker compose -f deploy/docker-compose.yml \
#                  -f deploy/overlays/docker-compose.adopt-volumes.yml up -d
# QUASAR_POSTGRES_VOLUME=deploy_quasar-nv-postgres-data
# QUASAR_AGENT_VOLUME=deploy_quasar-nv-agent-data
# QUASAR_CONTROL_VOLUME=deploy_quasar-nv-control-tls

# ── Rust / GStreamer (normally not needed to change) ──────────────────────────
# RUST_LOG=info

# ── Encrypted secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# QUASAR_SECRET_KEY itself moved up to "Strongly recommended" near the top of
# this file. This section is just the rotation knob and the full explanation.
#
# UNSET IS SUPPORTED AND IS THE DEFAULT: the control plane boots normally, every
# env-var credential below keeps working, and the admin UI reports that storing
# credentials is unavailable on this deployment. Nothing is generated for you —
# a generated key would diverge across nodes and make a backup unrestorable.
#
# BACK THIS UP WITH YOUR DEPLOYMENT SECRETS. If you lose it, every value stored
# through the admin UI is UNRECOVERABLE and must be re-entered. A wrong key is
# reported specifically ("the master key does not match"), never as "not
# configured". See docs/configuration.md §"Encrypted secrets".
#
# Decrypt-only predecessors for key rotation, comma-separated "<version>:<b64>".
# Rotating: put the OLD key here and a NEW key in QUASAR_SECRET_KEY above, then
# restart — existing rows keep decrypting under the old key while new writes use
# the new one. Re-save each secret through the admin UI to migrate it onto the
# new key; nothing does this automatically.
# QUASAR_SECRET_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# ── Cover artwork (UI-P7) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SHIPS OFF. With no API key the control plane makes no third-party request and
# every app renders its gradient tile — exactly the behaviour before this
# feature existed. Upload/override in the admin app editor works regardless.
# READ THE PROVIDER'S TERMS FIRST — docs/configuration.md §"Cover artwork".
#
# The key can ALSO be set from the admin UI (app editor -> Artwork), which stores
# it encrypted and applies it with no restart. A key stored there takes
# precedence over this variable; clearing it there falls back to this. Setting
# this remains fully supported and is unchanged for existing deployments.
# QUASAR_STEAMGRIDDB_API_KEY=
# QUASAR_ARTWORK_PROVIDER=steamgriddb   # or "none" to disable lookups outright
# Cache root. Must be a PERSISTENT volume — never point this inside web/dist,
# which is rebuilt (inode-swapped) on every web deploy.
# QUASAR_ARTWORK_DIR=/var/lib/quasar-control/artwork
# QUASAR_ARTWORK_MAX_BYTES=8388608
# QUASAR_ARTWORK_SWEEP_INTERVAL=15m

# --- Debug / profiling listener (PROF-01, #388) -------------------------------
# pprof SHIPS ENABLED, bound to the container's loopback. 6060 is published in no
# compose file, so the only way in is a shell on the host:
#   docker exec quasar-control-plane wget -qO- 127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/heap > heap.pb.gz
# Leave this alone unless you mean to turn it off ("off" runs no listener) or the
# port collides with something else in the container namespace. Widening the bind
# address is NOT the way to reach it remotely — pull the profile over ssh.
# See docs/configuration.md §"Debug / profiling listener".
# QUASAR_PPROF_ADDR=127.0.0.1:6060
# Go soft heap ceiling. Soft: above it the GC works harder, it never OOM-kills.
# GOMEMLIMIT=1GiB
